WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:11.731
Today we are going to see how Tesla uses no
less than a simulated game world to train

00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:13.920
their self-driving cars.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.950
And more.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:20.999
In their AI day presentation video, they really
put up a clinic of recent AI research results

00:00:20.999 --> 00:00:25.179
and how they apply them to develop self-driving
cars.

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:30.409
And of course, there is plenty of coverage
of the event, but, as always, we are going

00:00:30.409 --> 00:00:32.520
to look at it from different angle.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.520
We’re doing it Papers style.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.860
Why?

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.950
Because after nearly every Two Minute Papers
episode where we showcase an amazing paper,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:49.250
I get a question saying something like “okay,
but when do I get to see or use this in the

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.260
real world?”.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.990
And rightfully so, that is a good question.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:59.239
And in this presentation, you will see that
these papers that you see here get transferred

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.630
into real-world products so fast, it really
makes my head spin.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.960
Let’s see this effect demonstrated by looking
through their system.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:14.399
Now, first, their cars have many cameras,
no depth information, just the pixels from

00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:19.450
these cameras, and one of their goals is to
create this vector space view that you see

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.719
here.

00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:26.590
That is almost like a map, or a video game
version of the real roads and objects around

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:27.799
us.

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:30.149
That is a very difficult problem.

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:31.749
Why is that?

00:01:31.749 --> 00:01:34.780
Because the car has many cameras.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.740
Is that a problem?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.340
Yes… kind of.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.649
I’ll explain in a moment.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:46.350
You see, there is a bottom layer that processes
the raw sensor data from the cameras mounted

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.959
on the vehicle.

00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:54.639
So here, in go the raw pixels, and out comes
more useful, high-level information that can

00:01:54.639 --> 00:02:00.569
be used to determine whether this clump is
pixels is a car or a traffic light.

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:06.740
Then, in the upper layers, this data can be
used for more specific tasks, for instance,

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.880
trying to estimate where the lanes and curbs
are.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.120
So, what papers are used to accomplish this?

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:20.510
Looking through the architecture diagrams,
we see, transformer neural networks, BiFPNs,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.140
and Regnet.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.860
All papers from the last few years.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.450
For instance, RegNet is a neural network variant
that is great at extracting spatio-temporal

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.800
information from the raw sensor data.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.170
And that is a paper from 2020.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.830
From just one year ago.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:44.060
Already actively used in training self-driving
cars.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.600
That is unreal.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.650
Now, we mentioned that having many cameras
is a bit of a problem.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.870
Why is that?

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.920
Isn’t that supposed to be a good thing?

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.740
Well, look!

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.670
Each of the cameras only sees parts of the
truck.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.500
So how do we know where exactly it is, and
how long it is?

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.180
We need to know all of this information to
be able to accurately put the truck into the

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.170
vector space view.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:17.570
What we need for this is a technique that
can fuse information from many cameras together

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:18.570
intelligently.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:25.340
Note that this is devilishly difficult due
to each of the cameras having a different

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:31.070
calibration, location, view directions, and
other properties.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:37.820
So who is to tell that a point here corresponds
to which point in a different camera view?

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:42.750
And this is accomplished through, yes…a
transformer neural network.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.290
A paper from 2017.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.040
So, does this multi-camera technique work?

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.710
Does this improve anything?

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.080
Well, let’s see!

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:59.430
Oh yes, the yellow predictions here are from
the previous single-camera network, and as

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:05.460
you see, unfortunately, things flicker in
and out of existence.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:06.550
Why is that?

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:12.600
It is because a passing car is leaving the
view of one of the cameras, and as it enters

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.560
the view of the next one, they don’t have
this correspondence technique that would say

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.440
where it is exactly.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.760
And, look!

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.590
The blue objects show the prediction of the
multi-camera network that can do that, and

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:34.490
things aren’t perfect, but they are significantly
better the single-camera network.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:40.710
That is great, however, we are still not taking
into consideration time.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.220
Why is that important?

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.240
Let’s have a look at two examples.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.400
One, if we are only looking at still images
and not take into consideration how they change

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:56.200
over time, how do we know if this car is stationary?

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.190
Is it about to park somewhere?

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.830
Or, is it speeding?

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:07.610
Also, two, this car is now occluded but we
saw it second ago, so we should know what

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.810
it is up to.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.140
That sounds great.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:17.580
And what else can we do if our self-driving
system has a concept of time?

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.930
Much like humans do, we can make predictions.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:27.990
These predictions can take place both in terms
of mapping what is likely to come, an intersection,

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.389
a roundabout, and so on.

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:36.940
But, perhaps even more importantly, we can
also make predictions about vehicle behavior.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.550
Let’s see how that works.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:45.730
The green lines show how far away the next
vehicle is, and how fast it is going.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.300
This green line tells us the real, true information
about it.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.169
Do you see the green?

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:53.169
No?

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:59.139
That’s right, it is barely visible, because
it is occluded by a blue line, which is the

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:02.180
prediction of the new video network.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:07.419
That means that its predictions are barely
off from the real velocities and distances,

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:09.740
which is absolutely amazing.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:15.500
And, as you see with orange, the old network
that was based on single images is off by

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.630
quite a bit.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:23.419
So now, a single car can make a rough map
of its environment wherever it drives, and

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:30.680
they can also stitch the readings of multiple
cars together into an even more accurate map.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:35.590
Putting this all together, these cars have
a proper understanding of their environment

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.240
and this makes navigation much easier.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.389
Look at those crisp, temporally stable labelings.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:45.389
It has very little flickering.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:53.120
Still, not perfect by any means, but this
is remarkable progress in so little time.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.560
And we are at the point where predicting the
behaviors of other vehicles and pedestrians

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.490
can also lead to better decision making.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:04.060
But, we are still not done yet.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.300
Not even close.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.300
Look!

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.010
The sad truth of driving is that unexpected
things happen.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:16.360
For instance, this truck makes it very difficult
for us to see, and the self-driving system

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.750
does not have a lot of training data to deal
with that.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.410
So, what is a possible solution to that?

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.650
There are two solutions.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.610
One is fetching more training data.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:35.130
One car can submit an unexpected event and
request that the entire Tesla fleet sends

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.729
over if they have encountered something similar.

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:44.419
Since there are so many of these cars on the
streets, tens of thousands of similar examples

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:51.050
can be fetched from them, and added to the
training data to improve the entire fleet.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.410
That is mind blowing.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:59.550
One car encounters a difficult situation,
and then, every car can learn from it.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.470
How cool is that?

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.310
That sounds great.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.000
So what is the second solution?

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.440
Not fetching more training data, but creating
more training data.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.000
What, just make stuff up?

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.750
Yes, that’s exactly right.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:22.660
And if you think that is ridiculous, and are
asking how could that possibly work?

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:28.440
Well, hold on to your papers, because it does
work… you are looking at it right now!

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:35.060
Yes, this is a photorealistic simulation that
teaches self-driving cars to handle difficult

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:36.390
corner cases better.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:42.760
In the real world, we can learn from things
that already happened, but in a simulation,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.220
we can make anything happen.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:50.760
This concept really works, and is one of my
favorite examples is OpenAI’s robot hand

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.810
that we have showcased earlier in this series.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:59.210
This also learns the rotation techniques in
a simulation, and it does it so well, that

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:04.710
the software can be uploaded to a real robot
hand, and it will work in real situations

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.600
too.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.480
And now, the same concept for self-driving
cars.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:11.480
Loving it.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.950
With these simulations, we can even teach
these cars about cases that would otherwise

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.880
be impossible or unsafe to test.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.140
For instance, in this system, the car can
safely learn what it should do if it sees

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:29.570
people and dogs running on the highway.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:35.430
A capable artist can also create miles and
miles of these virtual locations within a

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:36.740
day of work.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:43.670
This simulation technique is truly a treasure
trove of data, because it can also be procedurally

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:49.390
generated, and the moment the self-driving
system makes an incorrect decision, a Tesla

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:55.030
employee can immediately create an endless
set of similar situations to teach it.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:10:01.600
Now, I don’t know if you remember, we talked
about a fantastic paper a couple months ago

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:08.899
that looked at real-world videos, then, took
video footage from a game, and improved it

00:10:08.899 --> 00:10:11.390
to look like the real world.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:14.520
Convert video games to reality if you will.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.440
This had an interesting limitation.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:23.750
For instance since the AI was trained on the
beautiful lush hills of Germany and Austria,

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.110
it hasn’t really seen the dry hills of LA.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.940
So, what does it do with them?

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:37.800
Look, it redrew the hills the only way it
saw hills exist, which is, covered with trees.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.060
So, what does this have to do with Tesla’s
self-driving cars?

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:47.861
Well, if you have been holding on to your
papers so far, now, squeeze that paper, because

00:10:47.861 --> 00:10:50.110
they went the other way around!

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:51.500
Yes, that’s right!

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:58.130
They take the video footage of a real, unexpected
event where the self-driving system failed,

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:05.140
use their automatic labeler used for the vector
space view, and what do they make out of it?

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:08.170
A video game version!

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:09.750
Holy mother of papers.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:16.350
And, in this video game, it is suddenly much
easier to teach the algorithm safely.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:23.310
You can also make it easier, harder, replace
a car with a dog, or a pack of dogs, and make

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:29.529
many similar examples so that the AI can learn
from these “what if” situations as much

00:11:29.529 --> 00:11:30.670
as possible.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:33.100
So, there you go.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:38.529
Full tech transfer into a real AI system in
just a year or two.

00:11:38.529 --> 00:11:42.910
So, yes, the papers you see here are for real.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.050
As real as it gets.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:49.790
And yes, the robot is not real, just a silly
joke.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.100
For now.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.310
And two more things that make all this even
more mind-blowing.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:59.670
One, remember, they don’t showcase the latest
and greatest that they have.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:04.700
Just imagine that everything that you heard
today is old news compared to the tech they

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:06.390
have now.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:12.560
And two, we have only looked at just one side
of what is going on, for instance, we haven’t

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.709
even talked about their amazing Dojo chip.

00:12:16.709 --> 00:12:22.810
And if all this comes to fruition, we will
be able to travel cheaper, more relaxed, and

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:31.019
also, perhaps most importantly, safer.

00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:43.460
I can’t wait.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:57.550
I really cannot wait.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:15.980
What a time to be alive!

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:27.860
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

